**Goal** — Implement the no-fallback child plugin, including files, polling, deadlines and settlement.

**Motivation** — `initInteractionChain` returns null for headless + `cli` (`src/interaction/init.ts`), so a chat-launched run's tool approvals are denied `unavailable` and its triggers never reach a person.

**Approach** — **Child side (the launched `nax run` / `nax bot` process).** A hidden `--chat-asks <dir>` flag (constant `CHAT_ASKS_FLAG`) names the job's ask directory. When it is present, `initInteractionChain` builds a chain whose single plugin is `ChatAskPlugin` (name `"chat"`), even in headless + `cli`. This story builds the plugin, not the flag/init wiring.

`ChatAskPlugin` has no `init` of its own.

`send(request)`:
- `notify` → write `<key>.notice.json`;
- otherwise write `<key>.ask.json`.

`receive(requestId, timeout)` until `askedAt + timeout`:
1. every `CHAT_ASK_POLL_MS` read `<key>.answer.json`; a valid answer resolves the ask;
4. at the deadline the ask resolves `{ action: "skip", respondedBy: "timeout" }` (the value every caller already maps through its own fallback);
5. every resolution writes `<key>.settled.json`.

The fallback-related branches of this algorithm arrive in US-004. `_chatAskPluginDeps` owns timers and clock; clean up waits during cancel/destroy. Encode file timestamps as ISO but return the existing numeric `InteractionResponse.respondedAt`. Preserve chain.prompt's existing choose normalization.

**Scope** — In: new plugin (`name`, `send`, `receive`, `cancel`, `destroy`), no-fallback happy path and timeout, `test/unit/interaction/plugins/chat.test.ts` with fake timers plus a real chain integration assertion. Out: guard/failure refinements (US-003), fallback (US-004), initialization wiring (US-005).

**Interface** — The full constructor contract is retained; fallback behavior is filled in US-004:
```ts
export interface ChatAskPluginOptions {
  readonly dir: string;
  readonly fallback?: InteractionPlugin;   // already initialised by initInteractionChain
  readonly fallbackDelayMs?: number;       // default 60_000
  readonly logger?: Pick<Logger, "warn">;  // default getSafeLogger(); the F3-F5 warnings go here
}
export class ChatAskPlugin implements InteractionPlugin {
  readonly name: "chat";
  readonly fallbackDelayMs: number;
  constructor(options: ChatAskPluginOptions);
}
```

**Goal** — Add delayed Telegram/webhook fallback to ChatAskPlugin without altering chain cascade semantics.

**Motivation** — `InteractionChain.receive` cascades only on a thrown error and Telegram resolves timeouts instead of throwing, so "chat first, Telegram later" cannot be built by registering two plugins; the chat plugin owns the fallback order itself.

**Approach** — `ChatAskPlugin` wraps the plugin the project config names as its fallback when that plugin is not `cli` (Telegram or webhook); with `cli` there is no fallback.

`send(request)`:
- `notify` → write `<key>.notice.json`; when the chat is closed also `fallback.send(request)`.
- otherwise write `<key>.ask.json`; when the chat is closed and a fallback exists, start the fallback at once.

`receive(requestId, timeout)` until `askedAt + timeout`:
1. every `CHAT_ASK_POLL_MS` read `<key>.answer.json`; a valid answer resolves the ask;
2. when `fallbackDelayMs` has elapsed, or at any poll where the heartbeat is stale, and a fallback exists and has not started: `fallback.send(request)`, then `fallback.receive(requestId, remainingMs)`; a fallback response resolves the ask unless its `respondedBy` is `"timeout"`, `"destroyed"` or `"superseded"` (the fabricated `skip` responses Telegram and webhook emit), which count as no answer;
3. the first resolution wins; if the chat won, `fallback.cancel(requestId)` is called;
4. at the deadline the ask resolves `{ action: "skip", respondedBy: "timeout" }` (the value every caller already maps through its own fallback) and the fallback, if started, is cancelled;
5. every resolution writes `<key>.settled.json`.

F2: Child cannot write `<key>.ask.json` and has a fallback — the ask goes to the fallback at once; `receive` waits on the fallback only.
F4: `fallback.send` throws — warning; the child keeps waiting on the chat until the deadline.
F5: `fallback.receive` throws — warning; the child keeps waiting on the chat until the deadline.
F6: Heartbeat missing or older than `CHAT_ASK_STALE_MS` — treated as chat closed: the fallback starts at once.

Destroy the wrapped fallback during plugin destroy. Use fake timers and a stub fallback in `test/unit/interaction/plugins/chat-fallback.test.ts`. Warnings go to the supplied logger (default getSafeLogger()); a fabricated timeout must never win the race.

**Scope** — In: fallback options, race ownership, closed-chat notification forwarding, F2/F4/F5/F6. Out: chain cascade changes, factory/init wiring (US-005), parent cards/registry (US-010).

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface ChatAskPluginOptions {
  readonly dir: string;
  readonly fallback?: InteractionPlugin;   // already initialised by initInteractionChain
  readonly fallbackDelayMs?: number;       // default 60_000
  readonly logger?: Pick<Logger, "warn">;  // default getSafeLogger(); the F3-F5 warnings go here
}
export class ChatAskPlugin implements InteractionPlugin {
  readonly name: "chat";
  readonly fallbackDelayMs: number;
  constructor(options: ChatAskPluginOptions);
}
```

**Goal** — Build the parent run-asks registry with heartbeat polling, deduplicated human cards, answer writes and settlement cancellation.

**Motivation** — Today the child's own asks (triggers, tool approvals, the agent's clarifying questions, precheck and paused-story prompts) never reach the chat.

**Approach** — **Parent side (the `nax chat` process).** The job runner creates the ask directory and writes a first heartbeat before it spawns a `run`, `bot_review` or `bot_fix` job, and appends `--chat-asks <dir>` to the argv (never for `plan`). A new run-asks registry (`src/chat/jobs/run-asks.ts`) polls the ask directories of running jobs, refreshes their heartbeats, turns each new ask into a human card or an agent notice, writes answers, closes cards whose ask was settled elsewhere and turns notices into transcript lines. This story initially raises only human cards; notices/notes and routing are filled by US-011/013.

**Ask ids.** The registry names an ask `<jobId>/<key>` (for example `run-1a2b3c4d/0f3c9a1b2d4e5f60`), where `key` is `chatAskKey(request.id)`.

**Cards.** A human card for a `confirm` or `choose` ask is `asks.requestHostChoice({ tool: "nax-run__ask", summary: "<jobId>: <request summary>", reason: request.detail ?? request.summary, choices, fallback: "closed", signal })`. `choices` is `[{ id: "approve", label: "Approve" }, { id: "reject", label: "Deny" }]` for `confirm` and `request.options` mapped to `{ id: key, label }` for `choose`. An `input` ask uses `asks.requestHostQuestion({ text: "<jobId>: <request summary>" plus "\n" + request.detail when present, signal })`. The registry aborts `signal` when the ask settles elsewhere; a card that resolves `"closed"` (aborted, or the controller's own timeout) or `null` writes no answer.

The registry subscribes to `jobs.onChange`; a change that leaves at least one job running starts the tick if it is not running.

The registry tick runs through `_runAsksDeps.setTimeout` / `clearTimeout`; the handle is `unref()`-ed when it has that method, so a chat host left open in a test does not keep the process alive.

Poll at RUN_ASK_POLL_MS = 1000. Scan settled state before creating a card and deduplicate across ticks. Encode confirm as approve/reject, choose as choose+value, input as input+value, always with chat-user. Establish the whole interface now: peekNotice/takeNotice always undefined; takeNotes always []; answer returns the not-waiting error; noteUserTurn no-op. US-011 fills notes/failure/dispose refinements, US-013 fills agent members. New tests are `test/unit/chat/jobs/run-asks.test.ts` and `test/integration/chat/run-asks-round-trip.test.ts`; the latter uses a real plugin and real registry over one temp directory, not mocked protocol calls.

**Scope** — In: registry contract, _runAsksDeps tick, human cards, chat-user answers and real protocol seam. Out: transcript formats/failure/dispose refinements (US-011), route selection/agent queue (US-012/013), host/TUI composition (US-014).

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface RunAskReply { readonly answer: string; readonly reason: string }
export type RunAskResult = { readonly ok: true } | { readonly ok: false; readonly message: string };
export interface ChatRunAsks {
  peekNotice(): string | undefined;          // US-010: always undefined; US-013: agent queue head
  takeNotice(): string | undefined;          // US-010: always undefined
  takeNotes(): readonly string[];            // US-010: always []; US-011: queued lines
  answer(askId: string, reply: RunAskReply): Promise<RunAskResult>; // US-010: not-waiting error
  noteUserTurn(): void;                      // US-010: no-op; US-013: resets the agent streak
  onChange(listener: () => void): () => void;
  dispose(): void;
}
export interface RunAsksOptions {
  readonly outputDir: string;
  readonly jobs: Pick<ChatJobs, "running" | "get" | "onChange">;
  readonly chains: Pick<ChatChains, "isActive">;
  readonly asks: Pick<ChatAskController, "requestHostChoice" | "requestHostQuestion">;
  readonly now: () => Date;
  readonly logger: Pick<Logger, "warn">;
}
export function createRunAsks(options: RunAsksOptions): ChatRunAsks;
```

**Goal** — Add pure routing and prompt builders for chain-authorized agent answers with a bounded streak.

**Motivation** — Slice 2 left this gap on purpose (`2026-10-27-nax-chat-workflow-dispatch-slice2-design.md` 4.6): the launch card warns "this run has no channel for its own asks; they will time out".

**Approach** — **Who answers (user rulings 2026-10-29).** `routeRunAsk` returns `"human"` when any of: the job has no `chainId`; `chains.isActive(job.chainId)` is false (this also covers jobs re-attached after a chat restart, since chain state is session-local); `request.metadata.safety === "red"`; `request.metadata.trigger` is `"pre-merge"` or `"human-review"`; the agent has already been handed `MAX_CONSECUTIVE_AGENT_ASKS` (3) asks since the last user turn. Otherwise `"agent"`. The order does not matter: every condition yields `"human"` and only their absence yields `"agent"`.

**Agent answers.** An agent-routed ask is queued as a notice (built by `buildRunAskNotice`) and delivered as a turn when the chat is idle, ahead of job and task notices. The agent answers with the no-card tool `nax-jobs__answer_ask` (`ask`, `answer`, `reason`) or answers `escalate`, which turns the ask into a human card. An agent answer applies once: `allow-remember` is refused for the agent. The answer shows as a transcript notice with the reason. This story supplies pure text/routing only; registry and delivery arrive later.

`routeRunAsk` and `MAX_CONSECUTIVE_AGENT_ASKS` in a new `src/chat/jobs/run-ask-route.ts`; `buildRunAskNotice` in a new `src/prompts/builders/run-ask-builder.ts`, exported from `src/prompts/index.ts`; the `answerAsks` option of `buildJobsInstructions`. All pure. Tests in new `test/unit/chat/jobs/run-ask-route.test.ts` and `test/unit/prompts/builders/run-ask-builder.test.ts`, and new cases in `test/unit/prompts/builders/workflow-builder-chain.test.ts`.

Target: `buildJobsInstructions(options?: { readonly answerAsks?: boolean }): string`; with `answerAsks: true` a ninth, last line names `nax-jobs__answer_ask`; without it the output is unchanged.

The notice starts `[nax-jobs] run ask <askId>`, includes request summary, names the answer tool, and enumerates confirm approve/reject/escalate; tool approval allow/deny/escalate (never allow-remember); other choose option keys plus escalate; input free text plus escalate.

**Scope** — In: pure route, constant, prompt builder/export and optional instructions line. Out: queue/streak mutation (US-013), child guards (US-003), tool/TUI delivery (US-014), chain approval surviving chat restart.

**Interface** —
```ts
export function routeRunAsk(input: {
  readonly job: Pick<ChatJob, "chainId">;
  readonly request: InteractionRequest;
  readonly chainActive: boolean;
  readonly agentStreak: number;
}): "human" | "agent";
```
`buildJobsInstructions(options?: { readonly answerAsks?: boolean }): string`; `buildRunAskNotice` takes the ask id and request needed to produce the specified notice (no additional signature is prescribed by the spec).
